3. Primary buyer
Primary economic buyer: the school owner, correspondent, chairman or trust management.
This person controls expenditure and cares about:
Operational control
Parent reputation
Fee visibility
Management reporting
Staff efficiency
Vendor durability
Total cost and implementation risk
Mandatory co-buyer: the principal.
The owner can approve the purchase, but the principal determines whether teachers and administrators actually adopt the product. Do not close a contract that the owner supports but the principal quietly opposes.
4. Secondary influencers
Principal: operational sponsor and adoption authority.
School administrator: daily workflow owner and likely implementation champion.
Finance administrator/accountant: validates fees, payments, receipts and reconciliation.
Academic coordinator/class teachers: validate attendance, examinations and report-card workflows.
Teachers: determine day-to-day adoption.
IT coordinator or external IT vendor: evaluates migration, devices, connectivity and support.
Trustees/management committee: may approve larger annual contracts.
Parents: not usually buyers, but their activation and feedback prove customer value.
Students: end users whose experience matters, but should not drive the initial commercial pitch.
5. Top five customer pain-point hypotheses
These are hypotheses, not proven FutureSchool customer findings. Validate them during discovery.
The same information is entered repeatedly.
 Attendance, marks, notices, fees and parent records live in separate registers, files or applications.
Academic workflows lack control and traceability.
 Marks move through spreadsheets and messages, making review, approval, correction and final publication difficult to govern.
Parent communication is noisy but not reliable.
 WhatsApp and calls distribute information but do not provide structured, authorised, searchable school records.
Principals see problems too late.
 They lack one view of missing attendance, pending approvals, report-card readiness, teacher coverage and operational exceptions.
Administration consumes too much staff time.
 Manual receipts, reminders, report preparation, data reconciliation and repeated parent questions reduce time available for higher-value work.
Ask every school to rank these. Do not assume “fees” is the most urgent pain simply because it has obvious financial value.
6. Top five FutureSchool value propositions
Connected workflows, not isolated modules
 Information moves from the responsible staff member through review, approval, publication and authorised visibility.
Clear role ownership
 Subject teachers, class teachers, principals and administrators perform different controlled steps instead of sharing one unrestricted process.
Principal visibility by exception
 Leaders see what requires action rather than searching multiple registers and groups.
A structured parent experience
 Parents get one authorised view of attendance, homework, results, fees, receipts, PTMs and notices.
Controlled, modular implementation
 Schools can start with two or three high-value workflows rather than attempting a disruptive whole-school ERP replacement.
Only advertise security controls—tenant isolation, MFA, audit trails, backup and controlled exports—after they have been implemented and tested.
7. Recommended sales pitch
60-second pitch
“Most schools already have software, spreadsheets and WhatsApp. The problem is that the work between them is still manual. Attendance may be recorded in one place, marks collected in another, report cards prepared separately, and parents informed through messages and calls.
FutureSchool connects those workflows. A teacher completes the task, the appropriate school authority reviews or approves it, the principal sees exceptions, and parents see only the final authorised information.
We do not recommend replacing everything on day one. We first identify one or two costly workflows, run a controlled pilot and measure whether FutureSchool reduces work and improves visibility. If the pilot does not demonstrate value, the school should not proceed.”
Discovery-first opening
“Before showing you the product, could we understand how attendance, report cards and parent communication currently work—and which one creates the most repeated work or complaints?”
Call to action
“Let us conduct a 30-minute workflow assessment with the principal and administrator. We will then demonstrate only the two workflows most relevant to your school.”
