Validation sequence
Calculate the cost floor.
 Track hosting, storage, messaging, payment costs, AI, support and onboarding separately.
Use:
Minimum price = variable cost per student ÷ (1 – target gross margin)
Example: if variable cost is ₹8 and the target gross margin is 75%, the minimum viable rate is ₹32. If variable cost is ₹12, the minimum is ₹48.
Conduct 20–30 discovery interviews before final packaging.
 Ask about annual software spend, implementation charges, current vendor, renewal date and approval process—not only “Would you pay ₹40?”
Use transparent package proposals.
 Do not secretly quote arbitrary prices to similar schools. Change package, service level or founding-school credit explicitly.
Collect at least 15 qualified commercial decisions.
 “Qualified” means the buyer saw the relevant workflow, confirmed a problem and received a written proposal.
Measure paid behaviour.
 Verbal interest and demo praise do not validate pricing. Paid pilots and signed annual contracts do.
Diagnose objections correctly.
Cash-flow objection: adjust payment schedule.
Missing feature: evaluate the product gap.
Low perceived value: strengthen problem qualification and proof.
Competitor undercut: differentiate or reduce service cost—not automatically discount.
Small-school economics: apply minimum annual contract or implementation fee.
One established alternative publicly lists annual plans of $999, $1,399 and $1,699, although package scope, services and local implementation are not directly comparable to FutureSchool. Therefore, ₹30–₹50 per student per month cannot be justified by a module count alone.
