16. Biggest commercial risks
Insufficient differentiation
 Attendance, fees, reports and messaging are already standard competitor claims. Sell connected governance and measurable implementation outcomes.
Teacher adoption failure
 The owner may buy, but teachers determine usage. Pilot teacher workflows before whole-school rollout.
Customisation destroys margins
 One-off reports, imports and workflows can turn SaaS into a services business.
Pricing without proof
 ₹40 may be acceptable, too high or unnecessarily low. Only paid decisions establish the answer.
High onboarding/support cost
 Data cleaning, training and parent activation can consume the first-year contract value.
Trust deficit of a new vendor
 Schools will ask about data ownership, exit, backups, support continuity and founder/company durability.
Long and seasonal buying cycles
 Missing the school’s budgeting or academic-year window can delay a decision by months.
Connectivity and device variability
 Digital readiness must be assessed during qualification.
Trying to sell every module
 Product breadth increases demo complexity and implementation risk.
Scaling before renewal evidence
 Ten sales do not prove a SaaS business if adoption is poor or schools do not renew.
