19. Ninety-day go-to-market plan
Days 1–30: problem and message validation
Complete the plan above.
Identify the three most repeated pains.
Test ₹30/₹40/₹50 package logic.
Sign the first paid pilot.
Stop adding features that no active prospect requests.
Days 31–60: pilot validation
Run two or three controlled pilots.
Measure baseline versus final workflow time.
Track parent activation, teacher usage and support effort.
Document all product and implementation failures.
Add another 75–100 qualified prospects.
Standardise training and data-import templates.
Exit condition: at least one school confirms measurable value and enters annual-contract discussion.
Days 61–90: commercial proof
Convert successful pilots to annual contracts.
Target 2–4 paying schools in total.
Create the first factual case study only with permission.
Request two introductions from each satisfied customer.
Compare actual onboarding and support cost with ACV.
Establish renewal review and customer-success processes.
Decide whether ₹40 remains the default rate.
90-day go/no-go questions
Did at least two schools pay?
Did teachers continue using the selected workflows?
Was onboarding economically supportable?
Which package actually sold?
What objection stopped otherwise-qualified buyers?
Can a second person repeat training and implementation?
Is there evidence of renewal intent?
If these answers are weak, do not scale advertising or hiring. Refine the segment, workflow and offer.
20. The single most important next action
Within the next 21 days, complete 15 in-person or video discovery interviews with qualified school owners/principals—without leading with a full product demo.
In the next 48 hours:
Select one local geographic cluster.
Build a list of 30 schools scoring at least 7/10.
Request ten warm introductions.
Ask for a 30-minute workflow interview.
Record exact answers about attendance, report cards, fees, parent communication, current software, budget cycle and implementation concerns.
The immediate objective is not to “market FutureSchool.” It is to discover which painful workflow a qualified school will pay FutureSchool to improve. That evidence should determine the first pitch, pilot, package and product priorities.
